  "_meta": {
    "title": "Observation places, historical and modern names with coordinates",
    "scope": "The recurring capitals and observing places named in the record corpus: the successive Chinese capitals, ancient western observing sites such as Babylon, Nineveh and Alexandria, and the modern East Asian observatories. Each entry gives one set of coordinates together with the historical name, its romanisation and the modern name. Places mentioned once in passing are not collected here; their names stay with the record that mentions them.",
    "fields": "key = place identifier. name = historical name in zh-Hans, zh-Hant, en, fr, es, it, ja, given as a bare settlement name; en is the Latin-script form, which for Chinese places is the romanisation and elsewhere the common English name, and fr/es/it/ja appear only where they differ from en. modern = present-day name, same language keys, zh-Hans always present and the rest only where they differ from it (optional). aliases = historical variant names, an array per language (optional). since/until = the years between which the entry applies, in astronomical years, since inclusive and until exclusive (optional). lat/lon = position in decimal degrees.",
    "provenance": "Coordinates and Chinese historical names are drawn from the gazetteer that underlies the corpus; coordinates themselves are not subject to copyright, and the historical atlases consulted while building it are not redistributed here. Names in the other languages and all modern names are established by hand rather than transliterated mechanically, so that Constantinople maps to Istanbul.",
    "count": {
      "places": 164
    },
    "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0",
    "updated": "2026-07-27",
    "caveats": [
      "Names are bare settlement names. Chang'an is entered as Chang'an, without the dynasty, palace or district qualifiers that a source may attach to it.",
      "Coordinates are approximate and stand for the place as a whole, a capital being placed at its seat of government rather than at a known instrument.",
      "since and until are present only where one place changed name without moving, as at Beijing (Dadu, then Jingshi), Nanjing (Jiankang, then Yingtian) and Istanbul (Constantinople, then Istanbul). An entry without them applies throughout.",
      "Not every place named in the corpus has an entry here, and a few records carry coordinates that genuinely disagree with this table, Luoyang at 34.63 against 34.62 among them."
    ]
  },
